      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cave City began when James Perry’s 1798 land grant later sold to John Owens in 1811 for $190.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_City%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Knob City Land Company mapped Cave City with 80‑foot wide streets, dreaming it would become a resort town close to Mammoth Cave.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_City%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "When the first train rolled into Cave City in 1859, the Louisville & Nashville Railroad ferried visitors straight to Mammoth Cave.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_City%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Sink Hole Spring, a modest creek, was Cave City’s sole water supply during its early days.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_City%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 1870 Cave City storm tore through the area, killing more than 20 people and razing Prewitt's Knob.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_City%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Cave City lies only 10 miles west of Mammoth Cave National Park’s heart, serving as a gateway for cave adventurers.",
